  {
   "id": "KhnFiGTRkJ4J",
   "from": "Dennis Murphy",
   "author": "0c39e60ddb38622b",
   "date": "2014-06-01T06:16:47Z",
   "subject": "Re: rug uses bl default in ggplot but not qplot",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hi:\r<br>\r<br>I think the problem is that geom_histogram() takes 1D input, but by\r<br>default, geom_rug() expects 2D input.\r<br>\r<br>The default value of the sides = argument in geom_rug() is &quot;bl&quot;, which\r<br>means to apply rug plots in both the x- and y-directions. This is a\r<br>problem when the only mapped aesthetic is x and the y-value is\r<br>derived, in this case from stat_bin() (e.g., ..count.. or\r<br>..density..). I found that the following worked:\r<br>\r<br>library(ggplot2)\r<br>mydata &lt;- data.frame(x = rnorm(100))\r<br>qplot(mydata$x, geom = &quot;histogram&quot; ) + geom_rug()\r<br>\r<br>This is because a histogram layer was produced first, so geom_rug()\r<br>would &quot;know&quot; that the y-variable was derived and would adjust\r<br>accordingly. OTOH,\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>qplot(mydata$x, geom = c(&quot;histogram&quot;, &quot;rug&quot;))\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "appears to be using x as the input data object for both\r<br>geom_histogram() and geom_rug(). There is no problem with the\r<br>histogram, since it passes x to stat_bin() to compute the counts, but\r<br>geom_rug() appears to be expecting 2D input by default with sides =\r<br>&quot;bl&quot; when the actual input is 1D and I think that&#39;s responsible for\r<br>the error. If we make an adjustment in the qplot() call, it seems to\r<br>work:\r<br>\r<br>qplot(mydata$x, geom = c(&quot;histogram&quot;, &quot;rug&quot;), sides = &quot;b&quot;)\r<br>\r<br>You can use geom_rug() with or without sides = &quot;b&quot; and\r<br>geom_histogram() in different orders using ggplot() and no error is\r<br>thrown.\r<br>\r<br>There are several reasons why I prefer ggplot() to qplot() - I think\r<br>you&#39;ve given me another one to add to the list...\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>Dennis\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>On Sat, May 31, 2014 at 12:37 PM, Bob Muenchen &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">muen...@tennessee.edu</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi All,\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; I did a histogram with a rug in ggplot, and it worked fine:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; mydata &lt;- data.frame(x=rnorm(100))\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; ggplot(mydata, aes(x)) +\r<br>&gt; + geom_histogram() +\r<br>&gt; + geom_rug()\r<br>&gt; stat_bin: binwidth defaulted to range/30. Use &#39;binwidth = x&#39; to adjust this.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Then I did it using qplot and it said &quot;sides&quot; has no default value. The help\r<br>&gt; file says it&#39;s &quot;bl&quot;.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; qplot(mydata$x, geom = c(&quot;histogram&quot;, &quot;rug&quot;) )\r<br>&gt; stat_bin: binwidth defaulted to range/30. Use &#39;binwidth = x&#39; to adjust this.\r<br>&gt; Error: argument &quot;sides&quot; is missing, with no default\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; When I specify &quot;bl&quot; it works:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; qplot(mydata$x, geom = c(&quot;histogram&quot;, &quot;rug&quot;), sides = &quot;bl&quot; )\r<br>&gt; stat_bin: binwidth defaulted to range/30. Use &#39;binwidth = x&#39; to adjust this.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Is this a bug, or am I misunderstanding the code differences between ggplot\r<br>&gt; and qplot?\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Thanks,\r<br>&gt; Bob\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;&gt; sessionInfo()\r<br>&gt; R version 3.1.0 (2014-04-10)\r<br>&gt; Platform: i386-w64-mingw32/i386 (32-bit)\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; locale:\r<br>&gt; [1] LC_COLLATE=English_United States.1252  LC_CTYPE=English_United\r<br>&gt; States.1252\r<br>&gt; [3] LC_MONETARY=English_United States.1252 LC_NUMERIC=C\r<br>&gt; [5] LC_TIME=English_United States.1252\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; attached base packages:\r<br>&gt; [1] grid      stats     graphics  grDevices utils     datasets  methods\r<br>&gt; base\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; other attached packages:\r<br>&gt; [1] mapproj_1.2-2 maps_2.3-6    ggplot2_1.0.0 GGally_0.4.6\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; loaded via a namespace (and not attached):\r<br>&gt;  [1] colorspace_1.2-4 digest_0.6.4     gtable_0.1.2     labeling_0.2\r<br>&gt; MASS_7.3-31\r<br>&gt;  [6] munsell_0.4.2    plyr_1.8.1       proto_0.3-10     Rcpp_0.11.1\r<br>&gt; reshape_0.8.5\r<br>&gt; [11] reshape2_1.4     scales_0.2.4     stringr_0.6.2    tools_3.1.0\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "&gt; --\r<br>&gt; --\r<br>&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the ggplot2 mailing\r<br>&gt; list.\r<br>&gt; Please provide a reproducible example:\r<br>&gt; <a href=\"https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370722744000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2ib3i-_cxYvS8RQ1oBqCFe\">https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility</a>\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; To post: email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggp...@googlegroups.com</a>\r<br>&gt; To unsubscribe: email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a>\r<br>&gt; More options: <a href=\"http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370722744000&amp;usg=AOvVaw21WLYzFa3xa0Dwtwg50asI\">http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2</a>\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; ---\r<br>&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups\r<br>&gt; &quot;ggplot2&quot; group.\r<br>&gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an\r<br>&gt; email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a>.\r<br>&gt; For more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792370722744000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3eTLFobiVFEocXGoL68vdx\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.\r<br>"
    ]
